  { q: 'What does WebMCP stand for?', a: 'Web Model Context Protocol. The name borrows from the Model Context Protocol, and the specification says it shares that vocabulary of tools, schemas and parameters deliberately. Note the name collision in search results with the WebM video format, which is unrelated.' },
  { q: 'Which browsers support WebMCP?', a: 'Chrome and Microsoft Edge, both as origin trials, and both built on Chromium. Position requests were filed with Mozilla and WebKit on 28 May 2026. Mozilla has since closed its issue as "position: neutral", and WebKit\'s carries concern labels but no stated position, so Chrome\'s own platform tracker still records Firefox and Safari as no signal.' },
